=== layout: two-panels

# 1. Who Knew Treatment Was Available?

@left

These men took part in the U.S. Public Health Service study at Tuskegee. The researchers enrolled Black men without their informed consent and withheld treatment from men with syphilis.

Once penicillin became available, what did the researchers owe them?

@right

![Tuskegee study participants](assets/set7_people_early/tuskegee_participants.jpg)

@citation

[CDC, Tuskegee study](https://www.cdc.gov/tuskegee/about/index.html)  |  [Belmont Report](https://www.hhs.gov/ohrp/regulations-and-policy/belmont-report/read-the-belmont-report/index.html)  |  [Photo: National Archives](https://rediscovering-black-history.blogs.archives.gov/2022/02/23/a-long-overdue-apology/)

=== layout: one-panel

# 1. When the available treatment changed

@body

- The Public Health Service began the Tuskegee study in 1932 to observe untreated syphilis.
- By 1943, penicillin was the treatment of choice. Participants were not offered it.
- The study continued until 1972. A study team has to revisit consent and treatment when the facts change.

@citation

[CDC, Tuskegee study timeline](https://www.cdc.gov/tuskegee/about/timeline.html)  |  [The Belmont Report (1979)](https://www.hhs.gov/ohrp/regulations-and-policy/belmont-report/read-the-belmont-report/index.html)

=== layout: subsection

# 2. Public Perception

=== layout: bookend

# 2. Questions

@body

Two concerns raised in the original prompt are lack of trust and limited knowledge of the science.

- How do these two perceptions affect biotechnology? What can we do to improve the public's perception? What are some of the difficulties in predicting possible dangers of biotechnology?
- How have historical events shaped public trust in biotechnology? How might the media's portrayal of biotech developments impact public perception? Can you provide examples of how misinformation has influenced public opinion on biotech? What role do educational initiatives play in bridging the gap between scientific knowledge and public understanding?
- Can public perception influence funding and policy decisions in biotechnology?

@citation

Discussion prompt source: BIOL 480, Fall 2026 Set #7, p. 15.

=== layout: one-panel

# 2. Why People Disagree About Biotechnology

@body

- Someone may want more information, distrust the source, or disagree about who bears the risk.
- Who gives the information and what they disclose affect trust.
- People can agree about the biology and disagree about ownership, risk, or fairness.
- A headline can turn an early result into a claim that feels certain.

@citation

[National Academies, Communicating Science Effectively](https://nap.nationalacademies.org/read/23674/chapter/4)

=== layout: big-image

@image

![Public views of gene editing depend on purpose](assets/set7_ethics_evidence/gene_editing_views.drawn.svg)

@caption

Topic 2: A number from a poll needs the full question. These two questions also change who would receive the editing.

@citation

[Pew Research Center, *AI and Human Enhancement* (2022), p. 105](https://www.pewresearch.org/internet/wp-content/uploads/sites/9/2022/03/PS_2022.03.17_AI-HE_REPORT.pdf)

=== layout: two-panels

# 2. Two Headlines, One Result

@left

*What the experiment showed*

"Crop survived disease in a greenhouse trial."

@right

*What a headline claimed*

"New crop will end crop losses."

What would a grower need to see in the field?

@citation

[National Academies, Communicating Science Effectively](https://nap.nationalacademies.org/read/23674/chapter/4)

=== layout: one-panel

# 2. What the Poll Did and Did Not Ask

@body

- Pew surveyed U.S. adults Nov. 1-7, 2021. It asked whether they favored gene editing to treat a serious disease or health condition a person currently has, or to make a baby more physically attractive.
- The responses describe the people surveyed at that time. They do not settle whether a specific treatment is safe or ethical.
- Before using a poll, read the exact population, date, and wording.

@citation

[Pew Research Center, *AI and Human Enhancement* (2022), p. 105](https://www.pewresearch.org/internet/wp-content/uploads/sites/9/2022/03/PS_2022.03.17_AI-HE_REPORT.pdf)

=== layout: one-panel

# 2. A Neighbor Asks About the Greenhouse Result

@body

"The crop survived" answers one question. A neighbor asks what will happen to the insects in her field.

- Show what the researchers counted in the greenhouse.
- Ask which insects and seasons matter to her. That answer changes what a field study should measure.

@citation

[National Academies, Communicating Science Effectively](https://nap.nationalacademies.org/read/23674/chapter/4)

=== layout: two-panels

# 2. A Result Needs a Comparator

@left

*Press release*

"The new crop resisted infection."

@right

*Study record*

How many plants, which field conditions, which comparison crop, and how much infection?

@citation

[National Academies, Communicating Science Effectively](https://nap.nationalacademies.org/read/23674/chapter/4)

=== layout: one-panel

# 2. What Would Earn Your Trust?

@body

Imagine two teams publish the same crop result. One posts the comparison data. The other asks growers to trust a press release.

- Which result can another grower check?
- Both teams may be right about the plants. The difference is what a grower can verify before planting.

@citation

[National Academies, Communicating Science Effectively](https://nap.nationalacademies.org/read/23674/chapter/4)

=== layout: one-panel

# 2. The Trust Question Is Usually Specific

@body

"Do you trust biotechnology?" is too broad to act on.

- A grower may trust a disease assay and still want insect data before planting a new crop.
- A patient may trust a treatment study and still ask who can see genetic data.
- Ask which claim, evidence, decision, and institution the person means.

@citation

[National Academies, Communicating Science Effectively](https://nap.nationalacademies.org/read/23674/chapter/4)

=== layout: subsection

# 3. Scientific Misconduct

=== layout: bookend

# 3. Questions

@body

- What are the main forms of scientific misconduct as defined by the National Science Foundation (NSF)? How do data fabrication and falsification manifest in research, and what are their impacts?
- What are the motivators for scientists to commit misconduct? Is scientific misconduct more prevalent in certain fields of research? If so, why might this be the case?
- Are there cultural differences in how scientific misconduct is viewed or handled? Provide an example. What happens to a scientist that is caught in misconduct?

@citation

Discussion prompt source: BIOL 480, Fall 2026 Set #7, p. 17.

=== layout: one-panel

# 3. Fabrication, Falsification, and Plagiarism

@body

| Pattern | Example |
| --- | --- |
| Fabrication | Enter measurements for a sample you never ran. |
| Falsification | Delete inconvenient values so the results look stronger. |
| Plagiarism | Copy an explanation without crediting the author. |

@citation

[NSF, 45 CFR 689.1: Research misconduct](https://www.ecfr.gov/current/title-45/subtitle-B/chapter-VI/part-689/section-689.1)

=== layout: one-panel

# 3. The Definition Has Limits

@body

- The federal definition names fabrication, falsification, and plagiarism in proposing, performing, reviewing, or reporting research.
- A surprising result is not misconduct. A documented error also has a different meaning from an intentional change to the record.
- The records matter because they let an investigation distinguish those situations.

@citation

[NSF, 45 CFR 689.1: Research misconduct](https://www.ecfr.gov/current/title-45/subtitle-B/chapter-VI/part-689/section-689.1)

=== layout: one-panel

# 3. One Image, Two Experiments

@body

- A scientist labels one micrograph as results from two separate experiments.
- Compare the raw files with the published figure. Ask whether the error changes the result.
- A correction or retraction changes the scientific record. A misconduct finding requires an investigation.

@citation

[COPE, Retraction guidelines](https://doi.org/10.24318/cope.2019.1.4)  |  [NSF, 45 CFR 689.1: Research misconduct](https://www.ecfr.gov/current/title-45/subtitle-B/chapter-VI/part-689/section-689.1)

=== layout: big-image

@image

![A concern about a figure starts with the record](assets/set7_ethics_evidence/correction_record.drawn.svg)

@caption

Topic 3: The published panel is only one part of the evidence available during review.

@citation

[COPE, Retraction guidelines](https://doi.org/10.24318/cope.2019.1.4)  |  [NSF, 45 CFR 689.1: Research misconduct](https://www.ecfr.gov/current/title-45/subtitle-B/chapter-VI/part-689/section-689.1)

=== layout: two-panels

# 3. Hwang Woo-suk and Fabricated Stem-cell Results

@left

Hwang Woo-suk is holding Snuppy, the dog his team cloned. Investigators verified the dog's origin.

The same investigation found that the team's reports of patient-matched human stem cells were fabricated. What did the evidence about Snuppy actually establish?

@right

![Hwang Woo-suk holding Snuppy](assets/set7_people_early/hwang_woo_suk_snuppy.jpg)

@citation

[Seoul National University, Hwang investigation (2006)](https://en.snu.ac.kr/snunow/snu_media/news?bbsidx=71497&md=v)  |  [Photo: Hwang with Snuppy](https://www.koinuno-heya.com/famous/snuppy.html)

=== layout: two-panels

# 3. What Would Another Lab Need?

@left

*Claim*

Patient-matched stem-cell lines came from the reported donors.

@right

*Evidence to inspect*

Cell-line identity, donor records and consent, laboratory notebooks, and independent genetic tests.

@citation

[Seoul National University, Hwang investigation (2006)](https://en.snu.ac.kr/snunow/snu_media/news?bbsidx=71497&md=v)

=== layout: one-panel

# 3. The Duplicated Panel Was an Honest Mistake

@body

The author finds both originals. The same file was pasted twice during layout.

- Publish the correct panel and explain whether the conclusion changes.
- An honest error can damage a paper. The federal misconduct definition excludes honest error.

@citation

[NSF, 45 CFR 689.1: Research misconduct](https://www.ecfr.gov/current/title-45/subtitle-B/chapter-VI/part-689/section-689.1)  |  [COPE, Retraction guidelines](https://doi.org/10.24318/cope.2019.1.4)

=== layout: two-panels

# 3. The Dog and the Stem Cells

@left

*Snuppy*

DNA tests supported the claim that the dog was a clone.

@right

*Stem cells*

The investigation found no support for the claimed patient-matched stem cell lines. What would a match have looked like?

@citation

[Seoul National University, Hwang investigation (2006)](https://en.snu.ac.kr/snunow/snu_media/news?bbsidx=71497&md=v)

=== layout: one-panel

# 3. Does an Unusual Value Belong in the Data?

@body

Two students measure the same sample. One result looks much higher than expected.

- A documented instrument failure can justify excluding a measurement under a consistent rule.
- Deleting a valid measurement solely because it weakens the conclusion misrepresents the record. What record would let you tell which occurred?

@citation

[NSF, 45 CFR 689.1: Research misconduct](https://www.ecfr.gov/current/title-45/subtitle-B/chapter-VI/part-689/section-689.1)

=== layout: one-panel

# 3. The U.S. Oversight System Has a Workload

@body

The HHS Office of Research Integrity reported 713 allegations received in calendar year 2024. It opened 117 cases and recorded 119 total closures.

- An allegation is a report, not a finding that misconduct occurred.
- A closure can reflect an administrative decision, no finding, or a misconduct finding after investigation.

@citation

[HHS Office of Research Integrity, *Annual Report CY 2024*, p. 3](https://ori.hhs.gov/sites/default/files/2025-12/CY%202024%20ORI%20Annual%20Report_final.pdf)
